<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.5.1: Writing and Evaluating New Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00441</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.5.1: Writing and Evaluating New Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>69f7a23d-d07a-4c1c-a322-d20216bd388d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Activity</title>
<exercise>
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    <para id="para-00001">Look at the following equation: \(50 + 1 = 51\).</para>
    
    <para id="para-00002">Perform each of the following operations and answer these questions: What does each resulting equation look like? Is it still a true equation?</para>
    
    <para id="para-00003">1. Add 12 to each side of the equation.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
    
    <para id="para-00004">
      Enter your answers here:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00006">\(50 + 1 + 12 = 51 + 12\), or \(63 = 63\) (or equivalent). Yes, it is still a true equation. </para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Question 2-->
<exercise>
  <exercise>
    <problem id="prob-00002"/><solution id="sol-00002"/></exercise>
<problem id="prob-00003">
   2. Add \(10 + 2\) to the left side of the equation and 12 to the right side. 
   2. Add \(10 + 2\) to the left side of the equation and 12 to the right side. </problem><solution id="sol-00003">
      
      <para id="para-00007">Enter your answers here:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer:</para>
      
      <para id="para-00009">\(50 + 1 + 10 + 2 = 51 + 12\), or \(63 = 63\) (or equivalent). Yes, it is still a true equation. </para>
    
    </solution></exercise>
<!--Question 3-->
<exercise>
  <exercise>
    <problem id="prob-00004"/><solution id="sol-00004"/></exercise>
<problem id="prob-00005">
    <para id="para-00010">
      3. Add the equation \(4 + 3 = 7\) to the equation \(50 + 1 = 51\).</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00005">
      
      <para id="para-00011">Enter your answers here:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
      
      <para id="para-00013">\(50 + 1 + 4 + 3 = 51 + 7\), or \(58 = 58\) (or equivalent). Yes, it is still a true equation. </para>
    
    </solution></exercise>
<!--Question 4-->
<exercise>
  <problem id="prob-00006">
    <para id="para-00014">
      4. Write a new equation that, when added to \(50 + 1 = 51\), gives a sum that is also a true equation.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00006">
    
    <para id="para-00015">Enter your answers here:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00017">Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
    
    <list id="list-00001">
      <item>\(8 + 2 = 10\). Added to the original equation gives: \(50 + 1 + 8 + 2 = 51 + 10\), or \(51 + 10 = 61\), or \(61 = 61\).</item>
      <item>\(a + a = 2a\). Added to the original equation gives: \(50 + 1 + a + a = 51 + 2a\), or \(51 + 2a = 51 + 2a\).</item>
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Question 5-->
<exercise>
  <problem id="prob-00007">
    <para id="para-00018">
      5. Write a new equation that, when added to \(50 + 1 = 51\), gives a sum that is a false equation.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00007">
    
    <para id="para-00019">Enter your answers here:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00021">Your answer may vary, but here are some samples</para>
    
    <list id="list-00002">
      <item>\(3 + 2 = 7\). Added to the original equation gives: \(50 + 1 + 3 + 2 = 51 + 7\), or \(56 = 58\), which is false. </item>
      <item>\(a + 2 = a\). Added to the original equation gives: \(50 + 1 + a + 2 = 51 + a\), or \(53 + a = 51 + a\), which is false. </item>
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise>
</section>
</content>
